From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 01:28:02 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] feat(fog): apply native biome and command settings

Accept resolved fog snapshots through a connection-owned receiver. Select
the camera's received biome key from the snapshot, below command settings
and above pack-defined data defaults. Apply fixed and render-relative
air distances and colors through the atmospheric pipeline, preserving later
camera, vision and void modifiers and existing fluid/weather paths.

Retire state on disconnect or listener replacement and reject callbacks
from older generations. Removing the final command reveals the selected
biome instead of Java's 1,024-block distance and purple End sky. The native
qualified End-biome fixture supplies the fixed 0..32 red control.

Matching native 1.26.60.5 uniforms establish the living admitted End dragon
cap at 0..min(100, effective render distance), with or without commands.
Retain that override above the resolved air distances, preserve colors and
restore ordinary settings after death. Boss bars do not own actor lifetime.

Render End skies with the licensed world-selected grayscale texture and
native color multiplication, below server overrides. Decode off-thread and
upload during client ticks before render passes. Dispatch disconnect GPU
retirement to the client executor and check listener ownership inside the
task so an old disconnect cannot clear a replacement session.

Modulate Overworld fog by the current world clock before later vision
modifiers. The raw pack RGB override previously skipped native night
dimming. Native 1.26.60.5 Fancy controls with #204060 establish midnight
RGB (0.0075294, 0.0150588, 0.0338824), then (2/9, 4/9, 1) with night vision.
The old direct renderer instead returned raw RGB, then (1/3, 2/3, 1).
Daylight and twilight vectors protect the clock phase and channel factors;
other dimensions keep their existing color path.

Focused regressions cover resource/session retirement, empty commands,
medium boundaries and the observed dragon cap. Directional sunrise tint, engine-specific vanilla rules, exact transitions,
End sky motion/sampling, fluids and weather still need native comparisons.

Native 1.26.60.5 caps final fog by a distance D = R - margin, where R is
the render distance in blocks (chunks << 4 at 0x144e097c3 and 0x1453ba2ff)
and the margin is R/16 up to 64 blocks, 8 up to 80 and 16 above
(0x14535f070). Its scale factor is the frame constant 1.0 written at
0x144e0981b and 0x144e098d8. Render-relative settings multiply D, the end
is clamped to 0..D and the start to D - clamp((D - 40) / 2, 4, 96)
(0x1453714a0, applied at 0x1453743c7). The End dragon's 0..100 override
(0x145390137) passes the same clamp, so a 5-chunk view ends at 72 blocks.
A stricter end cap at 0x1453714a0 depends on a camera-block flag set at
0x1453762e4, likely a submerged camera, and remains unmodeled.

Native 1.26.60.5 resolves air fog per frame (0x145372970). It samples the
biome at 27 positions around the floored camera: the center, 12 blocks
along each axis and 8 along each diagonal, at dy 0, -3 and +3 (table
0x14f246c50). FogManager::calculateCurrentDistanceSetting (0x142148a80)
walks the layers from the top. Each layer adds the remaining weight times
its defined samples' sum over its sample count and passes the undefined
share down, so a single command still wins outright and partial biome
coverage mixes with the data default. Settings mix in blocks. The data
default comes from the fallback lambda 0x1421c9ad0. Without one, native
fills the rest with a zeroed setting (0x152945670, written once by the
static initializer 0x1421ccd30): black, start and end 0. Partial biome
coverage then darkens and shortens the fog. With no defined layer at all
we keep Java fog, since that usually means the world's fog data has not
arrived. The clock-modulated result
then moves 1% toward the target each frame (0.01 at 0x14f0b7770, used at
0x145373dd8), before the distance cap and the dragon override. The first
frame and a medium change snap (0x145373cd6). We also snap after any frame
that used Java fog, such as fluid or rain frames.

Rain and thunder frames still use Java fog. Native picks the medium per
frame (0x145383570): water 2, lava 3, powder snow 5, otherwise weather 1,
or air 0 when the dimension byte +0x1a7 is set. For weather it resolves
the air and weather stacks and blends them by the Weather float at +0x4c
(Dimension+0x1e8, 0x145373218). That float is not the rain level, which
moves 0.01 per tick at +0x34/+0x38 (0x145e27530). It also darkens the sky
by 1 - 6.1/16 per unit (0x142938180). Its writer and the meaning of
+0x1a7 are not yet established.

BREAKING CHANGE: The fog receiver requires the matching core payload version 2.

